\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:efficiency}{{1}{5}{Paired mean-width ratios in all core distributions ($N=10n$), with 95\% Monte Carlo error bars. Left: nonlinear cross-fitting versus unrestricted PPI++; this is an asymptotic-tier comparison, not an assertion of matched finite-sample coverage. Right: exact two-stratum intervals versus labeled exact binomial intervals, both with finite-sample guarantees. The two panels use different references and vertical scales. Only the strongly informative fixed partition improves the exact comparator}{figure.caption.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:efficiency@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][4][]5}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{tab:asym}{{1}{5}{Asymptotic-tier comparison at $n=2000,N=20000$. Width ratios are ratios of mean widths against unrestricted PPI++; parentheses give paired Monte Carlo standard errors. Coverage is a fraction targeting 0.95; smaller width is preferable only subject to validity. The Step width gain accompanies an unadjusted coverage warning}{table.caption.2}{}}
\newlabel{tab:asym@cref}{{[table][1][]1}{[1][4][]5}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:validity}{{2}{6}{Coverage with Wilson 95\% Monte Carlo intervals; the dashed line is 0.95. Rare-event failures invalidate treating normal-based width gains as automatically valid. Exact procedures can be strongly conservative; reported coverage below 0.95 in an individual exact-binomial cell is sampling uncertainty, not evidence against the exact theorem. All-cell results, including unsafe in-sample fitting and oracle controls, appear in the appendix and CSVs}{figure.caption.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:validity@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][4][]6}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{tab:exact}{{2}{6}{Finite-sample-valid comparison at $n=2000,N=20000$. Both procedures have analytical coverage guarantees for binary outcomes under the stated iid design. The paired ratio is exact-stratified width divided by labeled-only exact-binomial width; smaller is narrower. Its Monte Carlo standard error is in parentheses. Coverage targets 0.95; no dominance across distributions is obtained}{table.caption.4}{}}
\newlabel{tab:exact@cref}{{[table][2][]2}{[1][4][]6}{}{}{}}
